---
title: The markup strip on its own
summary: strip_markup_ts removes TypeScript from a Svelte component's template, for tools that ship Svelte source another way. Run it on the output of preprocess(), when the script is JS but the template still has types.
related:
  - tsdown-plugin-svelte/how-it-works
  - tsdown-plugin-svelte/overview
---

`strip_markup_ts` is the second step of the plugin's build, exported for tools that ship Svelte source another way. Run it on the output of Svelte's `preprocess()`, when the script is already JS but the template still has types:

```ts
import { preprocess } from 'svelte/compiler';
import { strip_markup_ts } from '@ogygia/tsdown-plugin-svelte';

const { code } = await preprocess(source, preprocessors, { filename });
const plain_js = strip_markup_ts(code, filename);
```

It parses the file with Svelte's own parser, as TypeScript, and cuts out every type-only span in the template: `as` and `satisfies` casts, `!` assertions, annotations and generic arguments. It also removes `lang="ts"` from each script. Strings and comments that only look like types are left alone, because nothing is matched by pattern. `filename` is passed to the parser, for its error messages.

The `lang="ts"` attribute is how it knows the template is TypeScript: `preprocess()` leaves it on the script, and a file without it is returned unchanged. Everything that is not a type stays byte for byte as it was.
